 {"id": "DOC-10", "title": "P4 \u2014 DOCS AUDIT (tick duckbrain-docs 2026-10-04): the Host guard (--allowed-hosts / DUCKBRAIN_ALLOWED_HOSTS) is documented nowhere a reader can find", "priority": "P4", "status": "pending", "complexity": 2, "created_at": "2026-10-04T00:30:00+00:00", "reasoning": "DOCS AUDIT (tick duckbrain-docs 2026-10-04, HEAD e8e9b2c). The Host guard is shipped (12ef5ff feat(http): add --allowed-hosts / DUCKBRAIN_ALLOWED_HOSTS to the Host guard) and live in code: src/cli/http.ts:85 comment documents the env var; tests at src/cli/http-allowed-hosts.test.ts. Command run: grep -rn \"ALLOWED_HOSTS|allowed-hosts\" README.md docs skills .env.example -> 0 hits (verified 2026-10-04). An operator who exposes the daemon past loopback has no discoverable reference for the flag, the env override, or the empty-list behavior (empty/absent keeps the guard off per src/cli/http.ts:85). Artifact: docs/guide/deployment.md (exposure/auth section) + a DUCKBRAIN_ALLOWED_HOSTS row in docs/guide/configuration.md env table + one README line. Accept: grep -rn 'ALLOWED_HOSTS' docs README.md >= 2 hits describing flag + env + empty-list semantics, matching src/cli/http.ts behavior."}
 {"id": "TESTQ-DIRECT-ODIRECT-001", "title": "P2 \u2014 http-durability.test.ts asserts 201 for a direct-mode append without checking O_DIRECT support, so it fails on any host whose filesystem rejects O_DIRECT writes", "priority": "P2", "status": "complete", "complexity": 2, "created_at": "2026-10-04T01:30:54.170360+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-04T01:30:54.170360+00:00", "created_by": null, "detail": {"fingerprint": "26ec2f62a0276d500f2f4eecd0f036781cfa12ec092c7a405af35364c7ee1c2a"}, "reasoning": "Found by running the suite on a fresh off-origin host (bunker agent db-ver-1003, dedi-2, Ubuntu 24.04, kernel 6.8). The suite failed exactly one test and it is not a product defect \u2014 the test asserts a filesystem CAPABILITY as if it were product behaviour.\n\nThe failing assertion (reproduced standalone at main@aa6cc80, 5 passed / 1 failed):\n  src/cli/http-durability.test.ts > 'SUPA-1/SUPA-2: direct mode is framed through the serializer'\n  > 'returns 201 for the serializer-framed append and writes one memory'\n  AssertionError: expected 500 to be 201  (line 288)\n\nWhat the 500 actually is (the test never prints the body):\n  POST /api/memories to a namespace with durability.overrides = 'direct' returns HTTP 500 with\n  {\"error\":\"DURABILITY_UNSUPPORTED: O_DIRECT write rejected on <path>/raw_note/2026-10/current.jsonl (EINVAL: EINVAL: invalid argument, write) \u2014 write NOT acknowledged, no buffered fallback taken\",\"code\":\"DURABILITY_UNSUPPORTED\"}\n  Control in the same run: the identical POST to a non-direct namespace returns HTTP 201.\n\nSo the product is behaving CORRECTLY and loudly: it refuses rather than silently degrading to a buffered write, names the filesystem, and returns a structured code. Nothing is lost and nothing is misreported. src/storage/durability.ts:417-434 is where this is decided, and the message is deliberate ('no buffered fallback taken').\n\nThe defect is in the test: it hardcodes `expect(res.status).toBe(201)` with no capability probe, so it encodes 'this host can do O_DIRECT' rather than the contract. Evidence that it is environment-dependent, not a regression: the same file is green on the developer host and in CI (ubuntu-latest), and red on this host \u2014 while 'Run tests' PASSES in CI at the same commit.\n\nSharp edge worth capturing for whoever fixes it: on this host O_DIRECT OPEN SUCCEEDS and the WRITE fails EINVAL. Probed directly \u2014 open with O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_DIRECT returns a valid fd, then writing a 4096-byte buffer at offset 0 raises EINVAL (22). Both /tmp (/dev/md2) and /home (/dev/md3) are ext4 and behave identically. So a capability check that only attempts to OPEN would wrongly conclude O_DIRECT is available; the probe must attempt a real aligned WRITE.\n\nSuggested fix: make the test assert the CONTRACT rather than the capability \u2014 accept 201 (host supports O_DIRECT) OR 500 carrying code DURABILITY_UNSUPPORTED (host does not), asserting in the second case that no bytes were written and no buffered fallback was taken. Skipping the test entirely when the capability is absent would lose the coverage of the refusal path, which is the more interesting branch. Keep the direct-mode happy path covered wherever the capability exists.\n\nRelated: TESTQ-001 (no mutation/red-proof gate in CI) is the same theme one level up \u2014 this is a concrete case where a green-on-CI test is green for an environmental reason.", "worker_status": "complete", "commit_hash": "fbd0b6d", "verdict": "5e86805e"}
